CHAPTER 1
INTRODUCTION
1.1 Project Background and Motivation

Human resources constitute an important component of freight and logistics operations, where effective service delivery depends on employees performing their assigned responsibilities competently and consistently. Priority Handling Logistics, Inc., a company operating in the freight and logistics industry, conducts employee performance evaluation through an annual process using a standardized performance evaluation form. The evaluation covers employee performance during the designated evaluation period and provides information used in employee development and subsequent personnel-related decisions.

The organization's performance evaluation form assesses employees using ten performance factors identified as A through J. Each factor is evaluated using a five-point rating scale ranging from 1 – Poor to 5 – Excellent. The form provides separate assessment areas for the Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, and Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head. In addition to numerical ratings, the evaluation records conclusions, comments, recommendations, development information, career or transfer interests, and other information required for succeeding stages of the evaluation process.

The annual evaluation follows a defined sequence of participants and activities. The Employee/Ratee begins by completing the self-assessment. The assigned Immediate Supervisor or Rater then reviews the employee's assessment, provides independent ratings for the performance factors, and completes the required assessment and Step Two information. The Reviewing Supervisor or Division Head subsequently reviews the evaluation and completes the required Step Three information. The evaluation then proceeds to Personnel Office processing, Performance Evaluation Committee review, and President approval before the evaluation is finalized. This workflow reflects the structure of the organization's existing performance evaluation form and provides the basis for the proposed digital workflow.

Although the existing evaluation process provides an established method for assessing employee performance, maintaining and retrieving evaluation information through physical records can make historical performance review more difficult. Previous evaluation records may need to be manually located from employee files when HR personnel or supervisors need to examine an employee's performance history. This can make year-to-year comparison more difficult and increase the administrative effort required to retrieve and review records.

The sequential handling of evaluation documents also requires the evaluation to move between the Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head, Personnel Office, Performance Evaluation Committee, and President. A paper-based process can therefore limit visibility into the current status of an evaluation and increase the effort required to manage, route, and maintain evaluation documents. Written comments and recommendations stored within individual forms are likewise difficult to search and organize across employees and evaluation periods.

Another limitation is that information obtained through performance evaluation may be useful beyond the immediate evaluation process. The A–J performance factors can serve as indicators for identifying competency strengths and possible competency gaps. Development needs identified during evaluation may also lead to learning activities and training recommendations, while information concerning advancement potential, career interests, and qualifications may support succession-related planning. Recognition information can likewise contribute to an employee's overall development and performance history. In the existing paper-based process, these related areas are not organized through a single integrated digital structure.

Digital transformation in human resource management provides opportunities to improve the organization and accessibility of employee information. Research on human resource information systems indicates that digital HR platforms can reduce manual administrative work, improve the organization of personnel information, and support more efficient HR processes (Bangura, 2024). Similarly, research on digital transformation in human resource management emphasizes the value of integrated systems in supporting workforce information management and development activities (Destriani et al., 2024).

In response to these challenges, this capstone project proposes the development of a Performance and Development Human Resource Management System for Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. The system is primarily centered on the organization's annual performance evaluation. The evaluation serves as the main source of employee performance information, while competency management, learning and development, training management, succession planning, and social recognition function as supporting modules that use relevant evaluation information to support employee development and management activities. This design follows the principle that these functions are integrated with the performance evaluation process rather than operating as six separate HR workflows.

The proposed system also incorporates Gemini 2.5 Flash as an artificial intelligence-assisted decision-support component within the development portion of the evaluation process. Evaluation information, including employee and supervisor ratings, rating differences, identified strengths and weaknesses, and development information, may be analyzed to identify possible competency gaps and generate suggested development or training activities. The generated recommendations remain advisory. The Immediate Supervisor or Rater retains responsibility for reviewing, editing, accepting, regenerating, or rejecting AI-generated recommendations, and the AI does not modify official ratings or independently make employment decisions.

Through this integrated approach, the proposed system aims to establish a centralized digital source of employee performance and development information. The finalized evaluation can contribute to the employee's performance history, competency profile, development records, training information, career and advancement information, and recognition history within the Digital 201 File. This structure allows the annual performance evaluation to serve as the primary foundation for supporting employee development while providing authorized users with more organized access to historical information.

Overall, the project is motivated by the need to transform the organization's existing performance evaluation process into a more organized digital workflow while retaining the structure and responsibilities established by the existing evaluation form. Rather than replacing the evaluation process with unrelated HR functions, the proposed system uses the annual performance evaluation as its core function and connects its results to supporting competency, learning, training, succession, and recognition functions. This approach is intended to reduce manual record handling, improve access to historical performance information, support competency and development analysis, and provide a centralized foundation for employee performance and development management

1.2 Problem Statement

Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. currently conducts employee performance evaluation through an annual paper-based process. While the process provides a standardized method for evaluating employee performance, its manual nature creates several operational limitations in managing evaluation records and supporting employee development.

Historical Performance Data Accessibility

Historical evaluation records are maintained through physical employee files. When HR personnel or supervisors need to review previous evaluations, the corresponding documents must be manually located and retrieved. This makes it difficult and time-consuming to compare performance across multiple evaluation periods and identify recurring strengths or development areas.

Manual Evaluation Workflow and Documentation

The evaluation process requires documents to be completed and routed among the Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor or Rater, Reviewing Supervisor or Division Head, Personnel Office, Performance Evaluation Committee, and President. The paper-based process limits real-time visibility into evaluation progress and increases the administrative effort involved in document handling and record maintenance.

Rating Discrepancy Management

The evaluation form contains separate ratings from the Employee/Ratee, Supervisor/Rater, and Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head. Differences among these ratings may occur during the evaluation process. Under the existing paper-based process, these differences are not supported by a centralized digital mechanism for tracking and analyzing rating variations across employees and evaluation periods.

Unstructured Performance Comments and Development Information

Comments concerning employee strengths, weaknesses, development needs, career interests, and recommendations are recorded within evaluation documents. Because these records are maintained through individual paper forms, the information is difficult to search, organize, and analyze across employees or evaluation periods.

Fragmented Training and Development Records

Training and development needs identified during employee evaluation are not centrally connected to a digital record of employee development activities. Training participation, completion information, and related certificates may also be maintained separately, making it difficult to obtain a consolidated view of an employee's development history.

Limited Competency Gap Analysis

Although the evaluation process collects ratings for ten performance factors, the paper-based process does not systematically compare employee self-ratings with supervisor ratings to identify possible competency gaps. Consequently, development needs must largely be interpreted manually from individual evaluation forms.

Fragmented Employee Records

Performance evaluations, training information, development activities, and recognition records may exist in different physical documents or locations. The absence of a unified digital employee record makes it more difficult for authorized personnel to obtain a complete view of an employee's performance and development history.

These limitations reduce the organization's ability to efficiently retrieve historical performance information, organize employee development records, identify competency gaps, and support evidence-informed workforce development decisions. An integrated digital system can address these limitations by centralizing evaluation information, automating the evaluation workflow, and connecting evaluation results with related employee development functions.

1.3 Project Vision and Scope
1.3.1 Vision

The project envisions an integrated Performance and Development Human Resource Management System for Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. that digitizes and manages the organization's annual employee performance evaluation as its primary function. The system is designed to preserve the established evaluation workflow while providing a centralized digital platform for collecting, reviewing, processing, finalizing, and storing employee performance information.

The proposed system connects the results of the annual performance evaluation with supporting functions for competency management, learning and development, training management, succession planning, and social recognition. These supporting functions use relevant evaluation and employee information to organize development needs, competency gaps, training recommendations, career and advancement information, and recognition records rather than operating as separate HR workflows.

The system also incorporates Gemini 2.5 Flash as an artificial intelligence-assisted decision-support component for competency gap analysis and development or training recommendations. AI-generated recommendations remain advisory, while authorized supervisors and management retain responsibility and authority over evaluation, development, and employment-related decisions.

The overall vision is to provide authorized users with a centralized and organized source of employee performance and development information, including evaluation history, competency information, development activities, training records, succession-related information, and recognition records. These records are integrated into the employee's Digital 201 File to support more efficient performance management, employee development, and informed management consideration.

1.3.2 System Concept

The proposed system is centered on the organization's annual employee performance evaluation, which serves as the core function and primary source of employee performance information. The system digitizes the established evaluation process while maintaining its defined sequence of assessment, review, personnel processing, recommendation, approval, and finalization.

The core performance evaluation workflow consists of the following stages:

Employee/Ratee Self-Rating – The Employee/Ratee completes the required employee information and self-assessment for performance factors A through J and provides the required electronic signature.

Immediate Supervisor/Rater Assessment – The assigned Immediate Supervisor/Rater reviews the Employee/Ratee's assessment, provides independent ratings for performance factors A through J, and completes the required assessment, conclusions, comments, recommendations, development information, career or transfer information, and other Step Two requirements.

Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head Assessment – The Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head reviews the completed evaluation, provides the required assessment, comments, recommendations, and Step Three information, and provides the required electronic signature.

Personnel Office Processing – The Personnel Office records the required personnel and evaluation-result information, including applicable salary information, total points, adjective rating, and recommended increase or bonus.

Performance Evaluation Committee Review – The Performance Evaluation Committee reviews the completed evaluation and records the formal recommendation regarding the applicable personnel action or evaluation outcome.

President Approval – The President reviews the completed evaluation and Committee recommendation and provides the final approval or decision required for evaluation finalization.

Finalization and Record Storage – After the required approval is completed, the evaluation is finalized and retained as part of the employee's digital performance and personnel record.

The system's supporting modules are integrated with the core performance evaluation rather than operating as separate HR workflows. The A–J evaluation results provide the basis for competency information and possible competency-gap identification. Development needs may then be recorded through the Learning and Development function, while training needs and recommendations are maintained through Training Management. Information concerning advancement potential, career or transfer interests, qualifications, and development potential supports the Succession Planning function. Social Recognition maintains records of employee commendations and other recognized contributions.

Gemini 2.5 Flash functions as an artificial intelligence-assisted decision-support component within the development portion of the system. It may analyze authorized evaluation information, including Employee/Ratee and Supervisor/Rater ratings, rating differences, identified strengths and weaknesses, and development information, to assist in identifying possible competency gaps and generating suggested development or training activities. The Rater remains responsible for reviewing the generated recommendations and may use, edit, regenerate, or discard them. AI does not modify official ratings or independently approve promotions, salary changes, training assignments, or other employment decisions.

The system also provides role-based access control so that each authorized user can access only the evaluation information, supporting records, and functions appropriate to their assigned responsibilities. Electronic signatures and audit records provide evidence of completed actions and workflow activities. The resulting performance, competency, development, training, career and advancement, and recognition information can be organized within the employee's Digital 201 File, allowing authorized users to access relevant historical information in one centralized record.

1.3.3 In-Scope

The following functions and features are included within the scope of the project:

Annual Performance Evaluation. The system digitizes the organization's annual employee performance evaluation as its primary function, covering the complete workflow from Employee/Ratee self-assessment, Immediate Supervisor/Rater assessment and Step Two, Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head review and Step Three, Personnel Office processing, Performance Evaluation Committee recommendation, President approval, and finalization.
Digital Performance Factor Evaluation. The system supports the digital evaluation of performance factors A through J using the organization's five-point rating scale.
Multi-Level Performance Ratings. The system separately records the Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, and Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head ratings while preserving the information submitted at each evaluation stage.
Evaluation Comments and Supporting Information. The system records required conclusions, comments, recommendations, development information, advancement information, career or transfer information, and other evaluation data specified in the organization's performance evaluation form.
Performance Evaluation Workflow and Role-Based Access. The system manages evaluation stages and provides appropriate access for Employees/Ratees, Immediate Supervisors/Raters, Reviewing Supervisors/Division Heads, Personnel/HR personnel, Performance Evaluation Committee members, the President, and System Administrators.
Digital Signatures. The system supports electronic signature capture and storage for authorized participants involved in the evaluation workflow.
Evaluation History and Retrieval. The system maintains historical annual evaluations and allows authorized users to retrieve and review employee performance information across evaluation periods.

Supporting Modules
The following supporting functions use information generated from the core performance evaluation process:

Competency Management. The system uses A–J performance ratings as competency indicators to organize competency information, identify competency strengths and gaps, and support historical competency comparison.
Learning and Development Management. The system records employee development needs and development activities arising from identified evaluation requirements.
Training Management. The system records training needs, recommendations, participation, completion, and relevant training information resulting from evaluation and competency analysis.
AI-Assisted Development and Training Recommendations. The system uses Gemini 2.5 Flash to analyze authorized evaluation and competency information and assist in generating development and training recommendations. The recommendations remain advisory and are reviewed by authorized personnel.
Succession Planning. The system organizes advancement, career or transfer interest, qualification, and development-potential information captured through the performance evaluation to support management consideration.
Social Recognition. The system records employee commendations and other recognition as supporting employee-history information.
Digital Employee 201 File. The system consolidates authorized employee profile, performance, competency, development, training, career and advancement, and recognition information into a centralized digital employee record.
Reporting and Search. The system provides authorized users with searching, filtering, reporting, and retrieval capabilities for relevant employee performance and development information.
Audit Logging and Activity Tracking. The system records relevant system activities and workflow actions to support accountability and traceability.

1.3.4 Out-of-Scope
The following functions and features are outside the scope of the proposed system:

Recruitment and Applicant Management. The system does not include job posting, applicant screening, recruitment, selection, or hiring processes.
Payroll and Full Compensation Processing. The system does not perform payroll computation, salary disbursement, benefits administration, or full compensation management. Salary-related information may be recorded when required for performance evaluation and Personnel Office processing.
Timekeeping, Attendance, and Leave Management. The system does not manage daily time-in/time-out records, attendance monitoring, overtime computation, leave applications, or leave balances.
Disciplinary Case Management. The system does not manage formal disciplinary cases, investigation procedures, sanctions, or disciplinary documentation outside information required within the performance evaluation process.
Training Content Delivery. The system records training needs, recommendations, participation, completion, and certificates but does not host, create, or deliver training courses, instructional materials, or educational content.
Native Mobile Application. The proposed system is developed as a web-based application. Development of dedicated native Android or iOS applications is not included in the project scope.
Autonomous Human Resource Decision-Making. Artificial intelligence does not independently determine employee ratings, promotions, retention, salary adjustments, training requirements, or other personnel actions. AI-generated recommendations remain advisory and require review and decision by authorized personnel.
External HR System Replacement. The proposed system is intended to address the organization's defined performance evaluation and employee development requirements. It is not intended to replace every existing HR, payroll, accounting, or enterprise management system used by the organization.

The system may record or display information related to salary, training, employee records, and personnel processing when such information is required by the evaluation workflow. However, recording such information does not mean that the system performs the complete business processes associated with payroll, compensation, attendance, recruitment, or other excluded functions.

1.3.5 Target Users and Roles

The proposed system serves the following user groups:

Human Resources / Personnel Officer

The Human Resources or Personnel Officer manages employee profiles, annual evaluation cycles, evaluation progress, Personnel Office processing, historical evaluation records, and authorized reports. This role also oversees the movement of evaluations through the defined workflow and manages relevant employee performance and development information.

Employee / Ratee

The Employee or Ratee completes the required employee information and self-assessment for performance factors A through J during the authorized evaluation period. The employee provides the required electronic signature and may access information specifically permitted by the organization's defined access rules, including applicable finalized evaluation results.

Immediate Supervisor / Rater

The Immediate Supervisor or Rater reviews the Employee/Ratee's self-assessment and provides independent ratings for performance factors A through J. The Rater also completes the required Step Two information, including conclusions, comments, strengths, weaknesses, development information, advancement information, career or transfer information, and other recommendations. The Rater may review and manage AI-generated development and training recommendations by accepting, editing, regenerating, or discarding them. The Rater provides the required electronic signature upon completion of the evaluation stage.

Reviewing Supervisor / Division Head

The Reviewing Supervisor or Division Head reviews the completed evaluation after the Immediate Supervisor/Rater stage. This role completes the required Step Three information, including comments and recommendations, and provides the required electronic signature before submitting the evaluation to the Personnel Office.

Performance Evaluation Committee

The Performance Evaluation Committee reviews the completed evaluation and provides the formal recommendation regarding the appropriate personnel action or evaluation outcome. The available final actions include options such as retain, transfer, promotion, salary increase, training required, or other applicable action.

President

The President conducts the final review of the completed evaluation and Committee recommendation and provides the final approval or decision required to finalize the evaluation.

System Administrator

The System Administrator manages system users, roles and permissions, system configuration, evaluation configuration, system-level settings, and audit logs. This role is responsible for administrative functions rather than participating in the employee performance evaluation itself.

1.4 Objectives and Goals
1.4.1 General Objective

The general objective of this project is to design and develop a Performance and Development Human Resource Management System for Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. that digitizes and manages the organization's annual employee performance evaluation as its primary function, while integrating supporting functions for competency management, learning and development, training management, succession planning, and social recognition.

The system aims to improve the accessibility, organization, processing, and management of employee performance and development information through a centralized digital platform that supports role-based workflow management, digital signatures, audit tracking, historical performance records, and artificial intelligence-assisted competency gap analysis and development or training recommendations.

This objective is aligned with the final system design, which identifies the annual performance evaluation as the primary data source and the other required modules as supporting functions rather than separate HR workflows.

1.4.2 Specific Objectives
A. Digitize the Annual Performance Evaluation Process

To transform the organization's paper-based annual performance evaluation into a structured digital workflow covering Employee/Ratee self-assessment, Immediate Supervisor/Rater assessment, Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head review, Personnel Office processing, Performance Evaluation Committee review, President approval, and evaluation finalization.

B. Improve Historical Performance Accessibility

To provide authorized users with centralized access to employee evaluation records and historical performance information, allowing performance results to be retrieved, reviewed, and compared across evaluation periods.

C. Manage Multi-Level Performance Ratings

To record and preserve separate Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, and Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head ratings for performance factors A through J and provide structured information for reviewing rating differences.

D. Support Competency Gap Analysis

To use performance evaluation results, particularly relevant differences between Employee/Ratee and Supervisor/Rater ratings, to identify possible competency strengths and development gaps.

E. Provide AI-Assisted Development and Training Recommendations

To integrate Gemini 2.5 Flash as an artificial intelligence-assisted decision-support component that generates suggested development and training activities based on authorized evaluation information and identified competency gaps, while keeping the recommendations advisory and subject to human review.

F. Manage Employee Development and Training Records

To maintain centralized records of employee development needs, development activities, training needs, recommendations, participation, completion, and relevant training information arising from the performance evaluation process.

G. Support Succession and Career Development

To organize information concerning development potential, advancement outlook, career or transfer interests, and qualifications obtained through the performance evaluation to support succession and career-development activities.

H. Manage Employee Recognition Records

To maintain digital records of employee commendations, achievements, and other recognized contributions as part of the employee's development and performance history.

I. Consolidate Employee Performance and Development Records

To establish a unified Digital 201 File that organizes authorized employee profile, performance, competency, development, training, career and advancement, and recognition information in a centralized record.

J. Implement Role-Based Workflow and Access Control

To provide role-specific access to evaluation stages, employee information, supporting modules, reports, and system functions according to the responsibilities and authorization of each user.

K. Implement Digital Signatures and Auditability

To provide digital signature functionality and activity tracking that records relevant user actions, dates, and workflow events to support accountability and traceability throughout the evaluation process.

L. Provide Configurable Evaluation Management

To provide authorized administrators with appropriate mechanisms for managing configurable evaluation components, such as evaluation fields, factors, categories, and related settings, without requiring unnecessary modifications to the underlying application.

1.5 Significance and Relevance
1.5.1 Theoretical Significance

This study demonstrates the application of information systems principles to the digital transformation of an organization's annual employee performance evaluation and employee development process. It illustrates how a centralized digital system can structure the performance evaluation workflow, manage role-based access, preserve evaluation records, and connect performance information with supporting employee development functions.

The study also demonstrates the application of generative artificial intelligence as a decision-support mechanism within human resource management. Gemini 2.5 Flash is incorporated to assist in identifying possible competency gaps and generating development and training recommendations based on authorized evaluation information. The AI component does not modify official performance ratings or independently determine promotions, salary adjustments, training assignments, or other employment decisions. AI-generated recommendations remain subject to review and authorization by responsible personnel. This reflects the system design in which the Rater remains responsible for reviewing and deciding whether to use, edit, regenerate, or discard AI-generated recommendations.

The project further demonstrates how annual performance evaluation data can serve as the primary source for supporting related human resource functions. The proposed design does not treat performance management, competency management, learning and development, training management, succession planning, and social recognition as separate HR workflows. Instead, the annual performance evaluation provides the central performance information from which supporting competency, development, training, career, succession, and recognition records can be organized.

The study also demonstrates the use of a centralized employee record to organize performance and development information across evaluation periods. Through the Digital 201 File, authorized information concerning performance history, competency, development, training, career and advancement, and recognition can be maintained as part of a unified employee record.

Research concerning human resource information systems and digital transformation provides supporting context for the use of digital technologies in improving HR information management and organizational processes (Bangura, 2024; Destriani et al., 2024).

1.5.2 Practical Significance
For Priority Handling Logistics, Inc.

The proposed system provides Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. with a centralized digital platform for managing its annual employee performance evaluation process. It replaces the reliance on physical evaluation documents with a structured digital workflow that allows authorized personnel to monitor evaluation progress, access completed evaluations, and maintain organized historical records.

The system also connects performance evaluation information with supporting competency, learning and development, training, succession, and recognition functions. This provides the organization with a more organized view of employee performance and development information while maintaining the established responsibilities of each evaluation stage.

For Human Resources / Personnel

The system provides HR and Personnel users with centralized access to employee profiles, evaluation cycles, workflow status, historical evaluations, Personnel Office information, and authorized development records. This can reduce the administrative effort involved in locating physical evaluation documents and help organize the processing and monitoring of annual evaluations.

The Digital 201 File also provides a centralized location for authorized employee performance and development information, supporting more efficient retrieval and review of employee records.

For Supervisors and Managers

The system provides Immediate Supervisors, Reviewing Supervisors, and other authorized managers with structured access to the evaluation information required for their respective responsibilities. Supervisors can review current and historical performance information and record the required assessments, comments, and recommendations through the digital workflow.

The Immediate Supervisor/Rater may also use AI-assisted development and training recommendations generated from authorized evaluation information and identified competency gaps. These recommendations remain advisory, and the supervisor retains responsibility for reviewing, modifying, accepting, or rejecting the recommendations.

For Employees

The system provides employees with a structured digital mechanism for completing their annual performance self-assessment for performance factors A through J during the authorized evaluation period.

Employees may also receive controlled access to applicable finalized evaluation information according to the organization's access rules. This provides employees with appropriate visibility into their evaluation results while helping protect confidential supervisory comments and internal management information.

For Management Decision-Making

The system provides authorized management users with organized historical performance information, competency-related information, development needs, training recommendations, career and advancement information, and recognition records.

The availability of consolidated information can support management in reviewing employee performance and development patterns across evaluation periods. The system serves as a decision-support tool; it does not independently determine promotions, salary adjustments, training assignments, succession decisions, or other personnel actions.

For Future Researchers and Practitioners

The project provides a practical example of integrating a digital annual performance evaluation workflow with supporting employee development functions within a human resource management system. It also demonstrates the use of generative artificial intelligence as an advisory decision-support component while retaining human authority over performance and employment-related decisions.

The study may serve as a reference for future researchers and practitioners examining digital performance management, integrated HR information systems, competency development, AI-assisted HR decision support, and employee development management.
1.6 Definition of Terms

For clarity and consistency, the following terms are defined according to their use in this study.

Artificial Intelligence (AI). A technology used in the proposed system to analyze authorized evaluation information and generate recommendations that assist human decision-making. In this study, AI primarily refers to Gemini 2.5 Flash and its use in competency gap analysis and development or training recommendations.

Competency. The knowledge, skills, abilities, attitudes, and work behaviors relevant to an employee's effective performance of assigned responsibilities. In this study, competency-related information is represented through the organization's performance evaluation factors.

Competency Gap. A potential development area identified from differences between an Employee/Ratee's self-rating and the Immediate Supervisor/Rater's rating for a particular performance factor. The identified difference serves as an indicator for further review and does not by itself establish an actual competency deficiency.

Competency Gap Analysis. The process of examining relevant differences between Employee/Ratee and Immediate Supervisor/Rater ratings across performance factors A through J to identify possible areas for employee development.

Competency Management. A supporting system function that organizes competency-related information derived from performance evaluation data and supports the identification and monitoring of employee competency strengths and development areas.

Decision Support System. A system that provides information, analysis, or recommendations to assist human decision-makers without replacing their authority. In this study, AI-generated development and training recommendations serve as decision-support information for authorized personnel.

Digital 201 File. A centralized digital employee record containing authorized employee information, including performance evaluation history, competency-related records, development activities, training information, career and advancement information, and recognition records.

Electronic Signature (E-Signature). A digital representation of a user's signature used to certify the completion, review, or authorization of information within the evaluation workflow.

Employee / Ratee. The employee being evaluated through the organization's annual performance evaluation process.

Evaluation Workflow Stage. A defined stage in the sequential annual performance evaluation process. The stages include Employee/Ratee Self-Rating, Immediate Supervisor/Rater Assessment, Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head Assessment, Personnel Office Processing, Performance Evaluation Committee Review, President Approval, and finalization.

Finalized Evaluation. A completed evaluation record that has passed through the required evaluation stages and received the required final approval or authorization.

Freight and Logistics Industry. The industry sector involving activities such as freight handling, transportation, warehousing, storage, distribution, and related logistics operations.

Gemini 2.5 Flash. A generative artificial intelligence model developed by Google and integrated into the proposed system to assist with competency gap analysis and the generation of development and training recommendations.

Human Resource Management (HRM). The organizational activities involved in managing and developing employees, including performance management, employee development, training, and other workforce-related activities.

Human Resource Management System (HRMS). An information system used to manage and organize human resource information and processes. In this study, HRMS refers to the proposed Performance and Development Human Resource Management System for Priority Handling Logistics, Inc.

Immediate Supervisor / Rater. The supervisor directly assigned to evaluate an employee. This role provides independent ratings for performance factors A through J and completes the required assessment, Step Two information, comments, recommendations, and electronic signature.

Learning and Development. Activities intended to support employee knowledge, skills, professional development, and improvement, including coaching, mentoring, self-development, and other development activities recorded by the system.

Performance Evaluation. The formal annual process used by Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. to assess employee performance using the organization's standardized evaluation form and performance factors A through J.

Performance Evaluation Committee. The group responsible for reviewing completed employee evaluations and providing a formal recommendation regarding the appropriate personnel action or evaluation outcome.

Performance Management. The process of collecting, recording, reviewing, and managing employee performance information. In this study, performance management is primarily implemented through the digital annual performance evaluation workflow.

President Approval. The final approval stage of the evaluation workflow in which the President reviews the completed evaluation and Committee recommendation and provides the required final decision or authorization.

Reviewing Supervisor / Division Head. The authorized supervisor or division head who reviews the evaluation after completion of the Immediate Supervisor/Rater stage, provides the required Step Three information, comments, recommendations, and electronic signature, and represents a separate workflow role from the Immediate Supervisor/Rater.

Role-Based Access Control (RBAC). A security mechanism that controls access to system information and functions according to the user's assigned role and responsibilities.

Social Recognition. A supporting system function used to record employee commendations, achievements, and other recognized contributions as part of the employee's performance and development history.

Step 1 – Employee Self-Rating. The first evaluation stage in which the Employee/Ratee provides self-assessment ratings for performance factors A through J, completes the required information, and provides the required electronic signature.

Step 2 – Immediate Supervisor/Rater Assessment. The evaluation stage in which the Immediate Supervisor/Rater reviews the Employee/Ratee's self-assessment, provides independent ratings for performance factors A through J, completes the required conclusions, comments, recommendations, development information, career or transfer information, and other required assessment information.

Step 3 – Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head Assessment. The evaluation stage in which the Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head reviews the completed evaluation, provides the required Step Three comments and recommendations, and provides the required electronic signature.

Succession Planning. A supporting function that organizes information concerning employee development potential, advancement outlook, career interests, transfer interests, qualifications, and related development information to support organizational consideration of future positions.

Training Management. A supporting system function that records employee training needs, recommendations, participation, completion, certificates, and related training information.

Training Recommendation. A suggested training course, development activity, or learning intervention identified from evaluation information, competency-gap analysis, or AI-assisted analysis.

AI-Assisted Development and Training Recommendation. A development or training suggestion generated by Gemini 2.5 Flash from authorized evaluation and competency-related information. The recommendation is advisory and remains subject to review and decision by authorized personnel.

