CHAPTER 2
RELATED LITERATURE, STUDIES, AND TECHNICAL BACKGROUND
2.1 Review of Related Literature
Foreign Literature
Human Resource Management Systems

Human Resource Management Systems (HRMS) are information systems that use information technology to organize employee information and support human resource processes. Their adoption has increased as organizations seek to reduce manual work, improve information accessibility, and support more consistent HR operations. Raja et al. (2025), through a systematic review of HRIS and performance management, identified implementation, user, and organizational factors associated with the effectiveness of technology-supported performance management. Hussain et al. (2025) likewise examined HRIS in the tourism and hospitality sector and demonstrated the relevance of centralized HR information systems to organizational performance.

These studies establish the broader value of digital HR systems. In the proposed system, however, HRMS functionality is organized around the annual performance evaluation as the primary process, with competency management, learning and development, training management, succession planning, Social Recognition, and the Digital 201 File serving as interconnected supporting functions.

Performance Management and Employee Evaluation

Performance management involves the systematic assessment, monitoring, feedback, and development of employees. Digital performance management systems allow organizations to structure employee evaluations and maintain performance information for later review. Raja et al. (2025) found that HRIS and performance management are closely related areas and that technology can provide information and process support for performance management.

The proposed Performance and Development HRMS applies these principles through a defined annual evaluation workflow. It records independent A–J ratings from the Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, and Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head and carries the evaluation through Personnel processing, Performance Evaluation Committee review, President approval, and finalization. The emphasis of this function is therefore on the evaluation process and workflow, rather than on general HR data storage.

Competency Management and Competency Gap Analysis

Competency management focuses on identifying and developing the knowledge, skills, abilities, and work behaviors required for effective job performance. Estrella et al. (2024) examined competency-based recruitment and learning and development and emphasized the importance of aligning employee development with required competencies.

Competency gap analysis complements this process by examining differences among available competency or performance information. In the proposed system, differences among authorized employee and supervisor ratings are used as indicators of possible competency strengths or development areas. These differences do not automatically establish poor performance or a confirmed competency gap and instead provide information for further review and development planning.

Learning and Employee Development

Learning and development provide opportunities for employees to strengthen their knowledge, skills, and work capabilities through coaching, mentoring, self-development, and formal training. Da-ang et al. (2024) reported perceived benefits of training and development in areas including work quality, teamwork, professional development, and organizational goals. Tumacole (2024) likewise examined training and development practices and their relationship with employee job performance.

These findings support the Learning and Development function of the proposed HRMS. The system does not deliver learning content as a full learning management platform. Instead, it records development needs, activities, and related employee information identified through performance evaluation and competency analysis.

Training Management

Training management involves identifying training needs, organizing training recommendations, recording participation, and maintaining relevant training information. The proposed system connects these activities with employee performance and competency information. When an evaluation indicates a possible development area, related training needs or recommendations may be recorded and monitored.

This function is therefore distinct from Learning and Development. Learning and Development focuses on employee development information, while Training Management focuses on the planning and recording of training-related activities.

Succession Planning and Career Development

Succession planning considers employee potential, qualifications, development, and readiness for greater responsibilities. Raharjo et al. (2024) examined succession planning and job training in relation to employee performance, supporting the relevance of linking employee development information with future workforce considerations.

The proposed system records development potential, advancement outlook, career interests, transfer interests, and relevant qualifications captured during the performance evaluation. These records provide authorized personnel and management with organized information that may support future career-development and succession considerations. The system does not automatically select successors or approve promotions.

Social Recognition

Social Recognition provides a supporting record of employee achievements, commendations, and recognized contributions. Within the proposed system, recognition information is maintained as supplementary employee information and can form part of the employee's Digital 201 File. It is not used as an automatic ranking mechanism or as an independent performance evaluation process.

Artificial Intelligence in Human Resource Management

Artificial Intelligence (AI) is increasingly being applied to Human Resource Management, including performance management, training and development, and workforce analysis. Gélinas et al. (2022) identified AI applications across major areas of the HR life cycle while also highlighting legal and ethical considerations. Naoum et al. (2026) further emphasized the importance of transparency, accountability, fairness, and responsible implementation when applying AI in HRM.

The proposed system uses Gemini 2.5 Flash as an AI-assisted decision-support component. Authorized evaluation and development information can be analyzed to generate possible competency-related development and training recommendations. The AI does not determine official ratings, approve salary changes, decide promotions, finalize evaluations, or independently execute personnel actions. Final decisions remain with authorized human users.

Digital Employee Records and Centralized HR Data

Digital employee records support the organized storage and retrieval of employee information and historical records. Salah et al. (2022) demonstrated how HRIS can support HR management, performance evaluation, document management, reporting, and user management.

The proposed Digital 201 File applies this principle specifically to employee history. It consolidates authorized performance evaluations, competency information, development activities, training records, career-related information, and recognition history so that authorized users can retrieve an employee's information across evaluation periods.

Local Literature

Philippine studies further demonstrate the relevance of digital HR systems, employee development, and data analytics in local organizations. Salah et al. (2022) demonstrated how an HRIS can support human resource management, performance evaluation, document management, reporting, and user management. Da-ang et al. (2024) and Tumacole (2024) likewise highlight the contribution of training and development to employee work quality, competency development, and job performance. In addition, Paulino (2022) found that business analytics capabilities contribute to business intelligence and that business intelligence is positively associated with organizational performance.

Collectively, these local studies support the use of digital systems to organize employee information, support performance and development activities, and transform organizational data into useful information. However, the reviewed Philippine studies do not fully implement the specific evaluation-driven workflow proposed for Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. The proposed system addresses this gap by integrating the annual performance evaluation with competency management, learning and development, training management, succession planning, Social Recognition, and centralized employee records.

2.2 Analysis of Related Studies and Existing Systems
Google Sheets and Microsoft Excel

Google Sheets and Microsoft Excel are widely used spreadsheet applications for organizing, processing, and analyzing structured information. Spreadsheets are commonly used for business operations and data analytics because they provide functions for calculation, data organization, and chart development (Rebman et al., 2023). Google Sheets and Microsoft Excel also provide features such as formulas, filtering, charts, and PivotTables that can support the preparation of reports and basic data analysis (Google, n.d.).

For performance evaluation, spreadsheets can be used to record employee ratings, calculate scores, organize evaluation information, and prepare summary reports. However, these activities remain dependent on how users organize, maintain, and interpret the data. Spreadsheet applications are not specifically designed to manage a complete multi-stage performance evaluation workflow in which specific roles are assigned to each stage, previous submissions are preserved as read-only records, electronic signatures are associated with particular evaluation stages, and system rules control the movement of an evaluation between authorized users.

The proposed Performance and Development Subsystem addresses these limitations by incorporating the evaluation process directly into the system. Instead of relying on spreadsheets as the primary record and workflow mechanism, the proposed subsystem provides role-based access, separate A–J ratings for the Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, and Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head, digital signatures, controlled workflow transitions, centralized evaluation records, and historical retrieval.

Manual and Paper-Based Performance Evaluation

Manual and paper-based performance evaluation is a traditional approach in which employee ratings, comments, and other evaluation information are recorded through printed forms and manually processed. Dela Fuente and Dela Fuente (2022) described a performance evaluation process in which evaluation questions were provided through printed forms using a Likert scale, while overall performance measures were manually computed. The study demonstrates how traditional evaluation procedures can depend on physical documentation and manual processing of performance information.

Although manual evaluation can support a defined assessment procedure, handling multiple employees and evaluation stages may require considerable coordination. Evaluation forms must be distributed, completed, collected, reviewed, signed, and stored. Monitoring the current status of an evaluation may also require manual checking, while retrieving previous evaluations may require searching through stored physical records. Repeated handling of documents can also increase the need for manual verification and record maintenance.

The proposed Performance and Development Subsystem digitizes this process by maintaining evaluation information in a centralized system. Each evaluation is associated with an authorized user and workflow stage, allowing the system to track whether an evaluation is currently assigned to the Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head, Personnel Office, Performance Evaluation Committee, or President. Digital signatures, system-generated dates, workflow status, and audit information further support traceability throughout the evaluation process.

BambooHR

BambooHR is a cloud-based human resource management platform that provides performance management capabilities together with centralized employee information. Its performance management resources describe structured review cycles, goals, feedback, communication, scheduling, and performance-related processes intended to organize employee assessment within a centralized HR environment (BambooHR, n.d.).

These capabilities demonstrate the advantages of replacing disconnected employee records and manually managed performance processes with a centralized digital HR platform. A dedicated performance management environment can organize evaluation activities and provide a more consistent way of managing employee performance information.

However, BambooHR is a general-purpose HR platform intended to support a broad range of organizational requirements. The proposed Performance and Development Subsystem is specifically designed around the annual performance evaluation procedure of Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. It follows the required sequence of Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head, Personnel Office, Performance Evaluation Committee, and President. It also preserves the organization's specific A–J evaluation factors, role-specific editing permissions, digital signatures, and workflow rules.

SAP SuccessFactors

SAP SuccessFactors Performance and Goals is an enterprise performance management solution that supports performance management, goal management, continuous performance management, 360-degree reviews, and calibration. SAP documentation also identifies functions for employee self-evaluation, manager evaluation, competency-related assessment, and signing of performance review forms (SAP, n.d.-a; SAP, n.d.-b).

The platform demonstrates how performance management can be integrated with broader organizational talent and development processes. Its capabilities allow organizations to structure performance reviews, gather feedback from multiple sources, assess competencies, and standardize ratings through calibration (SAP, n.d.-a).

Despite these broad capabilities, SAP SuccessFactors is an enterprise-level HCM platform designed to accommodate diverse organizational processes. An organization may therefore need configuration to reproduce its own evaluation forms, rating factors, approval hierarchy, workflow sequence, and information-access requirements. The proposed Performance and Development Subsystem differs by concentrating specifically on the annual performance evaluation process of Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. and connecting its evaluation information to competency management, learning and development, training management, succession planning, Social Recognition, and the Digital 201 File.

Analysis of the Existing Systems

The reviewed approaches demonstrate different levels of support for employee performance management. Google Sheets and Microsoft Excel provide flexible tools for calculation, data organization, filtering, visualization, and reporting, but their use for performance evaluation remains dependent on user-managed data and processes (Rebman et al., 2023; Google, n.d.). Manual and paper-based evaluation provides a direct method of documenting performance but requires physical handling and manual processing, as demonstrated by Dela Fuente and Dela Fuente (2022). BambooHR demonstrates the use of a centralized HR platform for structured performance management, while SAP SuccessFactors demonstrates a broader enterprise approach that incorporates performance management, competencies, 360-degree reviews, and calibration (BambooHR, n.d.; SAP, n.d.-a).

The proposed Performance and Development Subsystem addresses these limitations by focusing on the specific annual performance evaluation requirements of Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. The system supports the complete workflow from Employee/Ratee self-assessment through Immediate Supervisor/Rater evaluation, Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head evaluation, Personnel processing, Performance Evaluation Committee review, President approval, and finalization. Separate A–J ratings are maintained for the Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, and Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head, while previously submitted information is preserved as read-only during later stages.

The proposed subsystem also extends the evaluation process into employee development. Authorized evaluation information can be analyzed to identify potential competency gaps and support development or training recommendations through Gemini 2.5 Flash. The AI component functions only as an advisory mechanism and does not independently modify official ratings, approve compensation, determine promotions, finalize evaluations, or execute personnel decisions. The subsystem further maintains a Digital 201 File containing relevant employee performance, competency, development, training, career, and recognition information for historical reference.

Overall, the reviewed systems demonstrate that digital tools can support employee information management, performance evaluation, reporting, feedback, and development. However, the proposed Performance and Development Subsystem is specifically designed around the workflow, evaluation factors, role responsibilities, approval sequence, and information requirements of Priority Handling Logistics, Inc. Its distinction lies in combining organization-specific multi-level performance evaluation with competency and development support, controlled workflow, digital documentation, centralized historical records, and AI-assisted development recommendations.

2.3 Agile Scrum Methodology

Agile development emphasizes iterative development, collaboration, adaptability, inspection, and continuous improvement. Scrum is an Agile framework that organizes development around incremental delivery through Sprints and provides defined events and artifacts for managing development work. The official Scrum Guide identifies the Sprint, Sprint Planning, Daily Scrum, Sprint Review, and Sprint Retrospective as Scrum events and defines the Product Backlog, Sprint Backlog, and Increment as Scrum artifacts (Schwaber & Sutherland, 2020).

Putrianasari et al. (2024) reviewed problems associated with adopting Agile-Scrum in small organizations and identified technical, organizational, process, and people-related factors affecting implementation. Their findings indicate that Agile-Scrum can provide adaptability but also requires coordination and suitable adjustment to the organizational environment.

The proposed Performance and Development HRMS uses an iterative approach because its components are interconnected and requirements may evolve during development. The Product Backlog can contain requirements related to performance evaluation, competency management, learning and development, training, succession, recognition, AI recommendations, administration, and system security.

During Sprint Planning, selected backlog items are prioritized for implementation. Development activities may include interface design, backend implementation, database integration, authentication, authorization, workflow transitions, AI integration, testing, and defect correction. Daily Scrum activities support coordination among the researchers, while Sprint Reviews provide opportunities to obtain feedback from relevant stakeholders.

The iterative approach is particularly valuable for the evaluation workflow because changes to one stage can affect other stages. For example, changes to evaluation fields, role permissions, database relationships, or workflow transitions may influence subsequent processing. Incremental implementation allows these dependencies to be tested and improved throughout development.

2.4 Emerging Technologies, Intelligent Systems, and Standards in Modern Information Systems

Modern information systems increasingly incorporate distributed architectures, artificial intelligence, analytics, cloud infrastructure, connected devices, cybersecurity controls, and software quality standards. These technologies are particularly relevant to enterprise environments that need to integrate multiple functional systems while maintaining performance, security, and maintainability.

The following technical discussions establish the concepts that support the broader freight and logistics enterprise environment and the specific Performance and Development Subsystem developed by the researchers.

2.4.1 Microservices Architecture

Microservices architecture is an architectural style in which an application is organized into relatively independent services with defined responsibilities and communication mechanisms. Söylemez et al. (2022), through a systematic literature review, described microservices as an architectural style that uses fine-grained services with independent lifecycles. The study identified advantages such as modularity, flexible configuration, easier development, easier maintenance, and productivity, while also emphasizing that microservices introduce substantial architectural and operational challenges.

Security is another important consideration in microservices. Almeida and Canedo (2022) explain that microservices increase the number of independently running components that need to be secured, making authentication and authorization important architectural concerns.

The broader freight and logistics enterprise system developed by the class is composed of multiple functional subsystems. The Performance and Development Subsystem is the HR-focused subsystem developed by the researchers. The group is responsible for its own application logic, database structures, interfaces, access controls, and related integrations while the subsystem participates in the broader enterprise architecture.

This structure should therefore be understood as a modular subsystem within the larger enterprise system. The existence of ten subsystems alone does not automatically establish microservices architecture because functional modularity and service deployment are not identical concepts. Microservices are characterized by service boundaries, independent execution or deployment, and defined communication mechanisms.

The detailed architectural implementation of the broader enterprise system, including service boundaries, communication mechanisms, and deployment decisions, is presented in the succeeding methodology and technical design sections.

2.4.2 Artificial Intelligence (AI)

Artificial Intelligence refers to computational technologies that perform tasks associated with human cognitive abilities, such as analysis, reasoning, pattern recognition, and information generation. AI has increasingly been applied to HR functions, including performance management, training and development, and workforce analysis. Gélinas et al. (2022) identified AI applications throughout the HR life cycle while emphasizing legal and ethical considerations.

Naoum et al. (2026) found that AI may improve efficiency and standardization in HR processes while creating concerns involving fairness, transparency, accountability, and responsible use.

Generative AI

Generative AI refers to AI systems capable of generating new text, explanations, summaries, recommendations, or other outputs from supplied inputs. In the proposed system, Gemini 2.5 Flash is used as a generative AI component for competency gap analysis and development or training recommendations.

The system provides the AI with authorized evaluation information, including relevant A–J ratings, rating differences, and development-related information. Gemini 2.5 Flash then generates recommendations intended to assist the Immediate Supervisor/Rater.

The AI component is limited to a decision-support role. It does not modify official employee ratings, approve salary changes, determine promotions, finalize evaluations, or independently assign training. Human users remain responsible for evaluating and acting upon the recommendations.

2.4.3 Internet of Things (IoT)

The Internet of Things (IoT) refers to interconnected physical devices capable of collecting, transmitting, and exchanging data through network technologies. IoT is particularly relevant to transportation, logistics, warehousing, and other operational environments that depend on connected devices and real-time information.

Idrissi et al. (2024), through a systematic review of blockchain, IoT, and AI in logistics and transportation, identified IoT and AI as important technologies in the transition toward smart logistics. The study notes that technology can support information access, analysis, automation, traceability, and operational efficiency in logistics systems.

IoT is therefore relevant to the broader freight and logistics enterprise environment. Connected vehicles, warehouse devices, tracking systems, and sensors may provide operational information that can eventually complement business and management systems.

However, IoT is not a core implemented function of the Performance and Development Subsystem. The subsystem primarily uses employee, evaluation, competency, development, and HR information. IoT is included in the technical background because it represents a relevant technology within the broader logistics environment and may provide opportunities for future enterprise integration.

2.4.4 Data Analytics and Business Intelligence

Data analytics involves examining data to identify patterns, trends, relationships, and useful information. Business intelligence focuses on organizing, presenting, and visualizing this information to support organizational decision-making.

Eboigbe et al. (2023) examined the relationship between business intelligence, AI, and data analytics and described how these technologies can transform organizational information into useful knowledge for operational and strategic decisions.

Paulino (2022) provides Philippine evidence by examining business analytics and business intelligence in the retail industry. The study found that business analytics capabilities contribute to business intelligence and that business intelligence has a positive relationship with organizational performance.

For the proposed HRMS, data analytics is applied primarily to employee performance and development information. The system can organize historical evaluation results, compare authorized rating differences, identify recurring indicators of competency needs, and summarize related learning and training records for authorized users.

The analytical results are intended to support human decision-making rather than replace it.

2.4.5 Polyglot Persistence

Polyglot persistence refers to using different persistence technologies or storage approaches according to the characteristics and requirements of different types of data. The concept is particularly relevant to distributed applications because different services may have different data requirements.

Microservices literature recognizes that services may use different technologies according to their individual requirements, but this flexibility also introduces additional operational and governance concerns. Söylemez et al. (2022) emphasize that distributed service architecture creates challenges that must be considered before adopting microservices.

Within the proposed enterprise environment, structured HR information such as employee profiles, A–J ratings, workflow states, role assignments, and evaluation results is naturally handled through relational data structures. Uploaded documents such as signed forms and training-related attachments may require file or object storage.

The project should only claim polyglot persistence as an implemented architecture when multiple distinct persistence technologies are actually used. Otherwise, the concept should be treated as a technical reference for handling different data types rather than as a claim that the subsystem uses multiple database engines.

2.4.6 Cybersecurity

Cybersecurity refers to the protection of information systems, networks, applications, and data against unauthorized access, alteration, disclosure, disruption, and other threats. HR systems require strong cybersecurity because they contain employee information, performance evaluations, development records, and management decisions.

The proposed system applies cybersecurity through authentication, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), restricted information visibility, workflow authorization, audit trail logging, and protected system functions. These mechanisms are important in a multi-role HR workflow because employees, supervisors, Personnel, Committee members, the President, and administrators require different levels of access.

Microservices-oriented environments also require careful authentication and authorization because independently running services increase the number of components that must be secured. Almeida and Canedo (2022) identified authentication and authorization as significant security considerations in microservice architectures.

2.4.7 Data Privacy

Data privacy refers to the responsible collection, processing, storage, use, and disclosure of personal information. Privacy is especially important in HR systems because employee information can affect employment, development, compensation considerations, and career opportunities.

In the Philippines, Republic Act No. 10173, or the Data Privacy Act of 2012, provides the legal framework for protecting personal information in government and private-sector information systems. The Act establishes principles for personal data protection and recognizes the responsibility of organizations to secure personal information. The Implementing Rules and Regulations further establish requirements related to personal-data protection and security.

The proposed HRMS applies privacy through role-based information access and controlled disclosure. Employees can access information authorized for them, while internal supervisor comments, confidential management information, committee deliberations, and other restricted information are not exposed to unauthorized users.

Privacy considerations also apply to the AI component. Only information necessary for the authorized AI-assisted analysis should be provided to the external AI service, with system permissions and data handling rules applied before information is processed.

2.4.8 Software Quality Standards

Software quality standards provide criteria for assessing whether a system satisfies expected functional and non-functional requirements. ISO/IEC 25010:2023 defines a product quality model for software and ICT products containing nine quality characteristics and corresponding subcharacteristics that can be used for specification, measurement, and evaluation.

The standard is relevant to the proposed HRMS because the system is intended for actual organizational use. Functional suitability requires the system to correctly support the annual evaluation workflow. Performance efficiency concerns acceptable system response and resource use. Reliability is important for protecting evaluation records. Usability is necessary because the system serves users with different responsibilities. Security is essential because employee information is sensitive, while maintainability supports future system changes.

The quality model therefore provides an appropriate foundation for evaluating whether the developed system meets both functional requirements and broader software quality expectations.

2.4.9 Cloud Computing

Cloud computing provides network-based access to computing resources such as storage, processing, and application services. Cloud-based HR systems can provide centralized access to employee information while reducing dependence on locally maintained infrastructure.

Maqueira Marín et al. (2022) conducted a systematic literature review of cloud computing in HRM and identified research concerning the development, adoption, implementation, and organizational implications of cloud-based HR platforms.

Cloud computing is relevant to the proposed HRMS because the system is implemented as a web-based application and may be accessed by authorized users through internet-connected devices. The exact deployment environment and cloud infrastructure used by the system are documented separately in the technical and deployment sections.

2.4.10 Edge Computing

Edge computing is a computing paradigm in which selected data processing activities are performed closer to the location where data is generated. It is useful in environments involving large numbers of connected devices and requirements for responsive processing.

Kong et al. (2022) examined edge computing in the Internet of Everything context and described its relevance to handling the large volume of data generated by connected devices. The study also identified challenges involving network, security, privacy, and edge-node implementation.

In freight and logistics environments, edge computing can be relevant to applications involving connected vehicles, tracking devices, warehouses, and operational monitoring. It may support lower-latency processing by reducing dependence on distant processing infrastructure.

Edge computing is not a core implemented component of the Performance and Development Subsystem. It is included as supporting technical background because it represents a relevant emerging technology for the broader logistics enterprise environment and a potential future integration capability.

2.5 DevOps Culture and CI/CD Practices

DevOps is an approach that promotes collaboration between development and operations, automation, continuous feedback, and reliable software delivery. It is particularly relevant to systems that require frequent development, testing, deployment, and maintenance.

Khattak et al. (2023) examined the adoption of DevOps culture and identified technological, cultural, and organizational challenges associated with successful implementation. Their findings demonstrate that DevOps requires coordination between technical processes and organizational practices.

Amaro et al. (2025), through a systematic literature review, mapped DevOps capabilities to software life-cycle processes and found that DevOps capabilities can contribute to improved software delivery, quality, and reliability.

Continuous Integration (CI) refers to the frequent integration and validation of code changes, while Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment supports predictable preparation and release of application updates.

For the proposed HRMS, CI/CD is relevant because changes to the evaluation workflow, database, access control, AI integration, or reporting functions may affect multiple system components. Automated build, test, and deployment processes can help detect issues earlier and support more controlled releases.

DevOps practices also complement Agile Scrum by providing operational practices for moving completed increments from development and testing toward deployment and maintenance.

2.6 Enterprise Architecture and System Integration

Enterprise architecture describes how organizational processes, information, applications, data, and technology are structured and integrated to support organizational goals. Martínez-López et al. (2024) describe enterprise architecture as an approach for dealing with the complexity of socio-technical systems and their information technology environments..

Enterprise architecture is relevant to the proposed project because the Performance and Development Subsystem is not developed as an isolated application. It is one component of a larger freight and logistics enterprise system composed of multiple interconnected subsystems.

Within the larger enterprise system, the researchers are responsible for developing the Performance and Development Subsystem. The subsystem includes its own application logic, database structures, interfaces, authentication and authorization, AI integration, and HR functions while remaining connected to the broader enterprise environment through defined integration points.

The modular organization allows each subsystem to maintain a clear functional scope. For the Performance and Development Subsystem, the annual employee performance evaluation serves as the central process. Its results are connected to competency management, learning and development, training management, succession planning, Social Recognition, and the Digital 201 File.

Integration does not imply that all users can access all data. Role-based permissions continue to determine which information can be viewed or modified. This allows the broader enterprise system to remain integrated while preserving confidentiality and functional separation.

The architecture also allows future integration with additional enterprise systems. Potential examples include payroll, attendance, operational, or other organizational applications. Such integrations remain outside the current project unless they are explicitly implemented and authorized.

2.7 Conceptual Framework

The conceptual framework of the study follows the Input-Process-Output (IPO) Model. The IPO model is appropriate for the proposed Performance and Development Human Resource Management System because it presents how employee and evaluation information enters the system, undergoes evaluation and analysis through the defined workflow, and produces outputs that support employee development and management activities.

Input. The input component consists of the employee and evaluation information required by the system. These include employee profile information, A–J ratings from the Employee/Ratee, Immediate Supervisor/Rater, and Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head, evaluation comments, development and career information, historical performance records, learning and training information, and recognition records. These inputs provide the information needed to conduct the annual performance evaluation and support subsequent development activities.

Process. The process begins with the Employee/Ratee completing the annual self-assessment, followed by the independent evaluation of the Immediate Supervisor/Rater and Reviewing Supervisor/Division Head. Authorized rating information may be compared to identify possible competency strengths or development areas. Gemini 2.5 Flash may then analyze authorized evaluation information and generate development or training recommendations for human review. The evaluation subsequently proceeds through the Personnel Office, Performance Evaluation Committee, and President according to the established workflow. Throughout the process, the system applies role-based access control, manages digital signatures, records workflow activities, and maintains evaluation history.

Output. The output component consists of the finalized performance evaluation and related employee development information. These include competency information, development and training recommendations, learning and training records, succession-related information, Social Recognition records, the Digital 201 File, and historical performance records. These outputs provide organized information that may assist authorized HR personnel and management in employee development and other appropriate workforce decisions.

